r","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Red onion","ingredient/red-onion"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"]]}]},"ingredient/pastry-cream":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pastry-cream","n":"Pastry cream","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Custard is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened milk, cheese, or cream cooked with egg or egg yolk to thicken it, and sometimes also flour, corn starch, or gelatin. Depending on the recipe, custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring sauce (crème anglaise) to the thick pastry cream (crème pâtissière) used to fill éclairs. The most common custards are used in custard desserts or dessert sauces and typically include sugar and vanilla; however, savory custards are also found, e.g., in quiche."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pastry-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastry_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine",[["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond flour","ingredient/almond-flour"],["Almond meal","ingredient/almond-meal"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fruit preserves","ingredient/fruit-preserves"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/500px-Custard.jpg","w":500,"h":458,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Interiot~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright…","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custard.jpg"}},"ingredient/crab":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/crab","n":"Crab","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Crabs are decapod crustaceans, either the Brachyura (the \"true crabs\") or various groups within the closely related Anomura (hermit crabs, porcelain crabs etc), characterised by having a heavily armoured shell, their tail segments concealed under the body, the ability to run sideways, and the habit of hiding in rocky crevices. They do not form a single natural group or clade, but have convergently evolved multiple times from the ancestral decapod body plan through carcinisation, the process of creating this set of characteristics. As a group, they are thus polyphyletic, meaning they have multiple evolutionary origins."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-crab"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Cambodian cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-cuisine",[["Kampot pepper crab","dish/kampot-pepper-crab"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Kampot pepper","ingredient/kampot-pepper"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Spring onions","ingredient/spring-onions"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/71/Cancer_pagurus.jpg/500px-Cancer_pagurus.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"© Hans Hillewaert","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cancer_pagurus.jpg"}},"dish/khanom-babin":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/khanom-babin","n":"Khanom babin","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Thai cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Khanom babin, also spelled as kanom babin, is a popular Thai dessert that comes from Ayutthaya. It is made from young coconut, rice flour, coconut milk, sugar and egg."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-khanom-babin"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khanom_babin"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Thai cuisine","links":[["Akhuni","dish/akhuni"],["American fried rice","dish/american-fried-rice"],["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"],["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Fish sauce","dish/fish-sauce"],["Green curry","dish/green-curry"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Khanom chin","dish/khanom-chin"],["Khanom thuai","dish/khanom-thuai"],["Khao soi","dish/khao-soi"],["La tiang","dish/la-tiang"],["Mi krop","dish/mi-krop"],["Miang kham","dish/miang-kham"],["Nam ngiao","dish/nam-ngiao"],["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"],["Pad see ew","dish/pad-see-ew"],["Pad thai","dish/pad-thai"],["Rad naa","dish/rad-naa"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Sweet chili sauce","dish/sweet-chili-sauce"],["Thai curry","dish/thai-curry"],["Thai fried rice","dish/thai-fried-rice"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Thua nao","dish/thua-nao"],["Tom kha kai","dish/tom-kha-kai"],["Tom yum","dish/tom-yum"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/41/%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B4%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%9920250309.jpg/500px-%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B4%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%9920250309.jpg","w":500,"h":329,"by":"ยุทธนาสาระขันธ์","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B4%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%9920250309.jpg"}},"dish/pavlova":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pavlova","n":"Pavlova","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#55b8ad","intro":[["Pavlova is a meringue-based dessert. Originating in either Australia or New Zealand in the early 20th century, it was named after the Russian prima ballerina Anna Pavlova. Taking the form of a cake-like circular block of baked meringue, pavlova has a crisp crust and soft, light inside. The confection is usually topped with fruit and whipped cream. The name is commonly pronounced   pav-LOH-və or (in North America)   pahv-LOH-və, and occasionally closer to the name of the dancer, as   PAHV-lə-və."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pavlova"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavlova"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine"],["New Zealand cuisine","cuisine/new-zealand-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Sucrose","ingredient/sucrose"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Belly Rumbles: Australian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"bellyrumbles.com","note":"Sara McCleary's collection of Australian classics such as Anzac biscuits, pavlova, sausage rolls and native-ingredient bakes.","url":"https://bellyrumbles.com/category/australian-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Eats","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipetineats.com","note":"Sydney-based Nagi Maehashi's widely used recipe site; broad in scope, but a staple of Australian home cooking.","url":"https://www.recipetineats.com/home/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Cloudy Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cloudykitchen.com","note":"New Zealand-born baker Erin Clarkson's blog, with a section of Kiwi classics.","url":"https://cloudykitchen.com/blog/category/new-zealand-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Australian cuisine","links":[["Anzac biscuit","dish/anzac-biscuit"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Flake (fish)","dish/flake-fish"],["Kangaroo meat","dish/kangaroo-meat"],["Parrot pie","dish/parrot-pie"],["Sausage sizzle","dish/sausage-sizzle"],["Steak and eggs","dish/steak-and-eggs"],["Zucchini slice","dish/zucchini-slice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/500px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG","w":500,"h":372,"by":"Hazel Fowler","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavlova_dessert.JPG"}},"dish/siu-mei":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/siu-mei","n":"Siu mei","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cantonese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Siu mei is the generic Cantonese name of meats roasted on spits over an open fire or in a large wood-burning rotisserie oven. This technique creates a unique, deep barbecue flavor and the roast is usually coated with a flavorful sauce (a different sauce is used for each variety of meat) before roasting. Siu mei is very popular in Hong Kong, Macau, Singapore, Malaysia and overseas Chinatowns, especially with Cantonese emigrants. In Hong Kong, the average person eats siu mei once every four days, with char siu being the most popular, followed by siu yuk (roast pork or pork belly) second, and roast goose third.  Some dishes, such as white cut chicken and soy sauce chicken, are not roasted at all but are considered siu mei nonetheless. Siu mei is also known colloquially as siu laap (燒臘), as the latter term encompasses siu mei and laap mei, a type of preserved meat. They are usu"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-siu-mei"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siu_mei"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Made With Lau","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"madewithlau.com","note":"Family-run channel and site teaching Cantonese recipes from retired chef Daddy Lau.","url":"https://madewithlau.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Cantonese cuisine","links":[["Cantonese restaurant","dish/cantonese-restaurant"],["Char siu","dish/char-siu"],["Har gow","dish/har-gow"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Lap cheong","dish/lap-cheong"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Sweet and sour pork","dish/sweet-and-sour-pork"],["Turnip cake","dish/turnip-cake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d4/HK_SYP_Ming_Sing_60402_16.jpg/500px-HK_SYP_Ming_Sing_60402_16.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. PX404~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright…","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HK_SYP_Ming_Sing_60402_16.jpg"}},"dish/tequeno":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tequeno","n":"Tequeño","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Venezuelan cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Tequeño is a fried spear of wheat dough stuffed with semi-hard queso blanco (white cheese), and is a popular meal or snack in Venezuela. To prepare it, the dough is wrapped around a cheesestick, formed into a breadstick and then fried or sometimes oven-baked. Tequeños can be eaten for breakfast, as an appetizer, or as a snack at parties and weddings. 21 October is the International Tequeño Day, and it was declared a cultural heritage of Venezuela on 20 April 2023."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tequeno"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teque%C3%B1o"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Venezuelan cuisine","cuisine/venezuelan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Queso blanco","ingredient/queso-blanco"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Enriqueta Lemoine: Venezuelan recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"enrilemoine.com","note":"Roundup of Venezuelan recipes on a Venezuelan-born food writer's blog.","url":"https://enrilemoine.com/en/2025/10/02/foolproof-and-fuss-free-authentic-venezuelan-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Venezuelan cuisine","links":[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4e/Teque%C3%B1os_v%C3%A9n%C3%A9zu%C3%A9liens_%C3%A0_Arepado_%28Lyon%29%2C_avril_2019_%282%29.jpg/500px-Teque%C3%B1os_v%C3%A9n%C3%A9zu%C3%A9liens_%C3%A0_Arepado_%28Lyon%29%2C_avril_2019_%282%29.jpg","w":500,"h":433,"by":"Benoît Prieur","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teque%C3%B1os_v%C3%A9n%C3%A9zu%C3%A9liens_%C3%A0_Arepado_(Lyon)%2C_avril_2019_(2).jpg"}}}